## 3. Validate & sanitize any future inputs (OWASP A03 – Injection)

Even though the current code has no request body, add a **global body parser** and use a validation library (e.g., `express-validator` or `joi`) for any new endpoints.

```bash
npm i express-validator
```

```js
const { body, param, validationResult } = require('express-validator');

// Example: creating a new user
app.post(
  '/users',
  express.json(),
  [
    body('name').isLength({ min: 2 }).trim().escape(),
    body('email').isEmail().normalizeEmail(),
  ],
  (req, res) => {
    const errors = validationResult(req);
    if (!errors.isEmpty()) {
      return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });
    }

    // safe to use req.body now
    const { name, email } = req.body;
    // ...insert into DB, etc.
    res.status(201).json({ id: 3, name, email });
  }
);
```

**Why?** Validation prevents SQL/NoSQL injection, command injection, and prototype pollution by ensuring the data conforms to an expected schema before it's processed.

